<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erfurt</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Erfurt is the capital of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, a city on the Gera first recorded in 742, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> founded a short-lived bishopric there. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the richest city of Thuringia, trading in woad, and kept wide self-government under the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. Its university, opened in 1392, taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1802.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erfurt.jpg" alt="Erfurt" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eremeev · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kr%C3%A4merbr%C3%BCcke,_Erfurt_6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>English scholars also glossed Latin texts, first in the margins or between the lines, then in word-lists such as the Épinal-<b>Erfurt</b>, Leiden and Corpus Glossaries, which grew into Latin–Old English glossaries with some of the character of dictionaries.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Martin Luther (1483–1546), a mining entrepreneur’s son from Eisleben, had abandoned the study of law in 1505 for the Augustinian friary at <b>Erfurt</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · People</a></li><li><blockquote>During a Hoftag held by King Henry VI at <b>Erfurt</b>, the floor of a building gave way under the combined weight of the assembled nobles and fell into the latrine cesspit below.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-26">26 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A council of patricians and later guild representatives ruled the city, which grew rich from cloth, woad and trade, joined the Hanseatic League and allied with <b>Erfurt</b> and Nordhausen in a Thuringian city league.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-muhlhausen">Free Imperial City of Mühlhausen</a></li><li><blockquote>It allied with <b>Erfurt</b> and Mühlhausen in a Thuringian city league and traded within the Hanseatic network.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nordhausen">Free Imperial City of Nordhausen</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1952 it was divided into the districts of <b>Erfurt</b>, Gera and Suhl; the state was re-established in 1990.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-thuringia">Free State of Thuringia</a></li></ul></section>
